   {
    "text": "The Potsdam Gravity Potato Explanation: Why do some places on Earth have higher gravity than others?",
    "url": "https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap141215.html",
    "refused": "SHAPE",
    "phase_int": 1,
    "concept": "A.GRAVITY",
    "Phi": 1.5891,
    "band": "Theta",
    "hz": 6.3564
   },
   {
    "text": "Since a center for studying these data is in Potsdam , Germany , and since the result makes the Earth look somewhat like a potato, the resulting geoid has been referred to as the P",
    "url": "https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap141215.html",
    "refused": "SHAPE",
    "phase_int": 2,
    "concept": "A.GERMANY",
    "Phi": 2.4588,
    "band": "Low alpha",
    "hz": 8.9175
   },
   {
    "text": "High areas on this map , colored red, indicate areas where gravity is slightly stronger than usual, while in blue areas gravity is slightly weaker.",
    "url": "https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap141215.html",
    "refused": "SHAPE",
    "phase_int": 1,
    "concept": "A.GRAVITY",
    "Phi": 1.56,
    "band": "Theta",
    "hz": 6.24
   },
   {
    "text": "Many bumps and valleys on the Potsdam Gravity Potato can be attributed to surface features, such as the North Mid-Atlantic Ridge and the Himalayan Mountains , but others cannot, an",
    "url": "https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap141215.html",
    "refused": "SHAPE",
    "phase_int": 1,
    "concept": "A.GRAVITY",
    "Phi": 1.5596,
    "band": "Theta",
    "hz": 6.2383
   },
   {
    "text": "Maps like this also help calibrate changes in the Earth's surface including variable ocean currents and the melting of glaciers .",
    "url": "https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap141215.html",
    "refused": "SHAPE",
    "phase_int": 1,
    "concept": "A.OCEAN",
    "Phi": 1.5672,
    "band": "Theta",
    "hz": 6.2689
   },
   {
    "text": "The above map was made in 2005, but more recent and more sensitive gravity map s of Earth were produced in 2011 .",
    "url": "https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap141215.html",
    "refused": "SHAPE",
    "phase_int": 1,
    "concept": "A.GRAVITY",
    "Phi": 1.555,
    "band": "Theta",
    "hz": 6.22
   },
   {
    "text": "NASA Hubble Mission Team Goddard Space Flight Center Jan 23, 2014 Article In this new Hubble image two objects are clearly visible, shining brightly.",
    "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/content/goddard/hubble-hubble-seeing-double/#.YpZyvYOZrRl",
    "refused": "SHAPE",
    "phase_int": 2,
